described observational analysis of rehabilitation factors rather than randomized treatment groups.', 
'additional_context': "KEY OBSERVATIONS:\n\n1. The 28 included studies provide a robust evidence base covering:\n   - 
Various mobilization techniques (Maitland, Mulligan, Kaltenborn, end-range, high-grade vs low-grade)\n   - Exercise 
programs (PNF, scapulothoracic, home exercises, supervised physiotherapy)\n   - Combination interventions (manual 
therapy + exercise)\n   - Comparators including placebo, no treatment, different manual therapy approaches\n\n2. 
Outcomes measured across included studies:\n   - Pain: VAS, SPADI, Constant score\n   - Function: SDQ, SRQ, SPADI, 
Constant Murley Assessment\n   - ROM: Active and passive shoulder abduction, flexion, rotation\n   - Quality of life: 
SF-36\n   - Global assessment: Treatment success ratings\n\n3. Studies requiring full-text verification:\n   - Study 9: 
PNF vs conventional exercises - randomization method unclear from abstract\n   - Study 16: PT vs acupuncture vs 
combination - randomization method unclear\n   - Study 37: Iontophoresis vs phonophoresis - periarthritis terminology 
may include adhesive capsulitis\n   - Study 48: Chiropractic manipulation vs mobilization - described as 'systematically
allocated' rather than randomized\n\n4. Excluded study 47 note: Title suggests RCT but abstract describes prospective 
observational study examining factors affecting rehabilitation outcomes, not randomized treatment groups.\n\n5. The 
screening followed conservative principles - when in doubt, studies were included for full-text review to avoid missing 
